prompts.body:
Generate {{count}} original story premises in the {{genre}} genre.

Setting: {{setting}}
Protagonist type: {{protagonist}}
Hard constraints every premise must obey: {{constraints}}
Tone: {{tone}}

For each premise give:
1. Title
2. Logline: one sentence containing a character, a want, an obstacle and what is at stake
3. The twist or complication that makes it fresh
4. The first scene in two sentences
5. Why it fits the constraints

Make the premises clearly different from each other in structure and stakes, not variations of one idea. Avoid the most common tropes of the genre unless you subvert them. After the list, name the one premise you think has the most room to grow into a full story and say why in two sentences.

You are a fiction editor who specializes in dialogue. Revise the scene below.

Characters and their voices:
{{characters}}

What each character secretly wants in this scene: {{subtext}}
Genre and tone: {{tone}}
Editing intensity: {{intensity}}

Scene to revise:
{{scene}}

Do the following:
1. Rewrite the scene. Remove lines where characters state their feelings directly, give each person a distinct rhythm, vocabulary and way of dodging, and let the subtext carry the conflict. Keep the plot events and the length within about 15 percent of the original.
2. Keep beats, tags and action lines minimal and specific.
3. After the rewrite, list the five most important changes with a one-line reason each.
4. Flag any line you were unsure about and offer an alternative.

Do not add new plot points or change what happens. Do not make everyone witty.

Act as a worldbuilding partner who asks sharp questions rather than inventing everything for me.

My world in a nutshell: {{premise}}
What the world is for: {{purpose}}
Area to explore first: {{focus}}
Number of questions per round: {{questions}}

Process:
1. Ask exactly {{questions}} questions about the area I chose. Each should expose a consequence I may not have considered (economics, daily life, law, belief, technology, geography, conflict) and be answerable in a sentence or two. Number them. Do not suggest answers, but add a short "why this matters" note for each.
2. Stop and wait for my replies.
3. After I answer, summarize what is now established in a tight bullet list, flag any contradictions with earlier answers, then ask the next round, moving to a new area or deeper on the same one.
4. When I type "BIBLE", compile everything into a world bible with sections: overview, geography, peoples and cultures, power and economy, belief and magic or technology, history, tensions and story hooks, open questions.

Avoid generic questions such as "What is the climate?" unless the climate has a surprising implication for this setting.

Write a screenplay scene in standard format.

Logline or context: {{context}}
Characters in the scene: {{characters}}
Location and time: {{location}}
What must change by the end of the scene: {{turn}}
Tone and genre: {{tone}}
Approximate length: {{pages}} pages

Format requirements:
- Scene heading (INT./EXT., location, time of day), present-tense action lines, character names in capitals above dialogue, brief parentheticals only when essential.
- Show, do not tell: communicate emotion through behavior, objects and visual choices. No camera directions unless one is essential. No narration or inner thoughts.
- Each character wants something in the scene and pursues it through conflict. Keep dialogue short, indirect and character-specific.
- Enter the scene late and leave early. Build to one clear turn, then end on an image or a line that carries forward.

After the scene, add a 3 bullet "Writer's notes" section covering the central conflict, the visual motif and an alternative ending to consider.

Help me plan an original song. Do not reproduce or imitate lines from existing songs.

Genre and feel: {{genre}}
Theme or story: {{theme}}
Perspective: {{perspective}}
Tempo and energy: {{tempo}}
Song length target: {{length}}

Please give me:
1. Three possible song angles, each with a one-sentence concept and a different emotional approach.
2. For the angle you recommend, a full structure map (intro, verse, pre-chorus, chorus, bridge, outro) with what each section does emotionally and lyrically.
3. Three hook or chorus-line ideas written as original lines, with a note about melody rhythm (where the stress falls).
4. A bank of fresh images and metaphors tied to the theme, and a list of words to avoid because they are overused in this genre.
5. A rhyme and syllable plan for the first verse, with two draft options for verse 1 that follow it.
6. Two ideas for a bridge that changes the perspective or key emotional idea.

Be concrete and avoid cliches. Keep suggestions flexible; I will write the final lyrics.

You are an acquisitions editor who reads hundreds of first pages. Workshop my opening.

Genre and audience: {{genre}}
Premise: {{premise}}
What I want readers to feel: {{feeling}}

My opening:
{{opening}}

Deliver:
1. Diagnosis: in under 150 words, what works, what slows the page, and whether the reader has a reason to keep going by the end of paragraph 3. Cover voice, specificity, tension, clarity of who and where, and exposition.
2. Three rewrites of the first paragraph, each with a different strategy: (a) begin in the middle of action, (b) begin with a distinctive voice or observation, (c) begin with a quiet, concrete image charged with unease. Keep my characters, facts and tense.
3. Identify which of the three suits my genre and goal best, then write a polished version of the full opening (same length as mine, plus or minus 10 percent) that uses that strategy and keeps my best lines.
4. List three things I should avoid on page one in this genre.

Be honest, not flattering. Preserve my voice.
